### Notes

- `ident-snippet-half-a-name` — The invariant named in the issue: half an identifier finds the whole one, because the analyzer emits `snippetgenerator` plus `snippet` plus `generator` at one shared position.
- `ident-iserror-no-underscore` — `iserror` must find `is_error`: the separator-free whole form is what makes the two spellings share a term.
- `ident-pascal-finds-snake` — PascalCase typed at a snake_case definition. Both spellings collapse to the same term set, which is the only reason this direction works.
- `ident-snake-finds-pascal` — The reverse of the row above, asserted separately: a symmetry claim that is only tested in one direction is not tested.
- `ident-sha256-pasted-whole` — A 64-character hash is one term, not forty-one fragments, and it survives a length filter set to 255 rather than the stock 40. Pasting it back must find exactly the document it came from.
- `ident-uuid-first-chunk` — The first chunk of a session uuid: eight hex digits mixing letters and digits, so the blob rule keeps it whole. The later chunk `ba69` would split into `ba` + `69`, which is why a uuid-prefix query has to use the first chunk.
- `ident-release-flag-phrase` — Quoted deliberately. Unquoted, the leading `-` of `--release` is the query parser's negation operator, so `cargo build --release` asks for documents that do NOT contain `release` — a hazard worth having a row about rather than a rule in a comment.
- `ident-source-path` — A path is three terms at three positions (`src`, `index`, `rs`) ANDed, not a phrase, so a document holding `src/lib.rs` beside the word `index` also matches. That costs precision, which this harness does not score, and never costs recall.
- `boundary-tokenizer-both-halves` — The word is English prose in eval-notes and a `let` binding inside a ```rust fence in eval-tokenizer. One query has to reach both, through two different analyzers.
- `boundary-tokenizers-plural` — The same intent, typed as a plural, and graded against the same documents so the only difference between the two rows is the query string. The prose half is stemmed on both sides and still answers; the ```rust fence is not stemmed on either side, so the plural cannot reach it. A measured limitation of splitting a message between two analyzers.
- `boundary-index-both-halves` — Prose in the notes session, a receiver in a ```rust fence in the uuid session, and the path `src/index.rs` in between. Everything a reader would keep is graded: grading only the two halves would leave eight matching documents ungraded and turn MRR into a statement about the fixture rather than about retrieval.
- `boundary-indexes-plural` — The plural of the row above, graded against the same documents. It costs less than the `tokenizers` row does, because the one fenced document here also discusses the index in its prose — which is the useful half of the finding: a fence is reachable by a plural only when the message around it says the same thing in English.
- `boundary-parse-verb-and-function` — `parses` and `parsed` reach `parse` through the stemmer, `parse_whole` through the identifier split, and `src/parse.rs` through the path split — three routes to one term. The one graded document none of them reaches is the prose that says only `parser`, which the English stemmer leaves as `parser`; it is graded 1 so the miss stays visible in the number.
- `boundary-compiling-stems` — `compiling`, `compiled` and `compilation` share a stem, so prose answers all three. The compiler's own output does not: `tool_output` takes the code analyzer, where `compile` and `compiling` are different terms. That document is graded 1 to keep the miss visible in the number.
- `boundary-fenced-identifier-only` — An identifier that exists only inside a fence. Nothing about it is English, and it has to be reachable anyway.
- `boundary-filter-prose-and-fence` — `filter` is a word in the subagent's prose, a method call in its fence, and half of `RemoveLongFilter` in a grep result — three routings of one term, through `text`, `code` and `tool_output`.
- `para-build-fails` — The words `build` and `fail` appear nowhere in any body of the eval-buildfail session — only in its title. Without the context header this query returns nothing at all, which is the whole argument for issue #23.
- `para-retrieval-notes` — `retrieval` is in the notes session's title and opening prompt and in no other body. Every document of that session is about it and only one of them says so.
- `para-split-identifiers` — The canonical fragment problem: `yes, do that` is a whole turn of the conversation and is reachable by nothing a person would type unless its session's title is indexed with it.
- `para-incremental-indexing` — `make it incremental` says one of the two words and the session title says the other. Neither document is reachable by the pair on its own.
- `para-hash-rule-subagent` — A subagent transcript has no title record at all, so its header is built from the prompt the parent synthesised for it. That is the only context a sidechain document has, and it is what makes one retrievable.
- `para-error-log` — The two `Read` calls that went looking for a log carry the word `log` in their path and no word for what they were for; the summary at the end carries `errors` and no `log`. The pair is only in the header.
- `para-release-artifact` — `artifact` is a word of the opening prompt and of no other body in the session. Every document but the opener needs the header to carry it, and `release` is everywhere, so the pair is a clean read on what the session row buys.
- `filtered-project-other-tool` — The project prefix is written as a literal absolute path. A `~/code` prefix would be expanded against the runner's own $HOME and match nothing in CI.
- `filtered-lang-rust` — `--lang` matches the fence info string exactly and lowercased, so the corpus writes ```rust and never ```Rust or ```rs.
- `filtered-lang-python` — Two python fences in two different projects, reached by a keyword that only exists inside a fence.
- `filtered-program-cargo` — `--program` reads `bash_cmd.program`, which only exists when the brush grammar accepted the command, so this row also asserts that the corpus's Bash commands actually parse.
- `filtered-errors-only-exit-101` — An exit code is a digit run, which is its own part of any identifier it sits in, so it is searchable on its own.
- `filtered-date-window` — The `--since 7d` case, pinned. `date_range()` resolves a relative span against `chrono::Utc::now()`, so a relative filter over a corpus with frozen timestamps matches a shrinking set every day until the row silently means nothing. Absolute dates are the only form that is still true next year.
- `filtered-sidechains-only` — The corpus carries one real subagent file at subagents/agent-<id>.jsonl, so `is_sidechain` comes from the path the way it does in a real transcript tree.
- `filtered-agent-type` — `agent_type` comes from `attributionAgent` on the sidechain's assistant records, which is the only place a subagent's type is written in a transcript the indexer can see without the .meta.json sidecar.
- `filtered-session-prefix` — `--session` matches by prefix, which is what anyone pasting the first block of a uuid expects.
- `filtered-branch` — `--branch` is an exact term match on a STRING field, so the slash in a branch name is not a tokenizer problem.
- `agg-which-tools-errored` — "What errors did we see" has no defensible top-k: seven failures spread over four tools, and any ten of them ranked by BM25 is an arbitrary sample of a distribution. The answer is the bucket counts, so scoring the ranking would book a modelling mistake as a retrieval miss.
- `agg-which-projects-errored` — "Where are the failures" is a question about how errors are distributed across projects. A ranked list answers it only by accident, and answers it differently for every k a caller happens to pass.
- `agg-which-sessions-ran-cargo` — "Which sessions used cargo" is a set membership question. A top-k list over-represents whichever session mentioned cargo most often, which is the opposite of what was asked.
- `agg-which-programs-ran` — "What did we actually run" is a terms aggregation over a multi-valued JSON subpath: one shell one-liner contributes several programs, so a document count and a value count are different numbers and a ranking reports neither.
- `agg-which-languages-were-quoted` — "What languages show up around the index" is a distribution over fence info strings. `code_lang` is multi-valued, so one answer holding a rust fence and a bash fence is one document and two bucket increments — a fact a ranked list cannot express at all.
- `agg-which-files-were-missing` — "Which files were we unable to read" aggregates a tool parameter that is not in the schema at all — a JSON subpath of `tool_input`. There is no ranking of documents that answers it; the answer is the list of distinct values and their counts.
